/**

@page page_exahype_amr Adaptive mesh refinement

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE solvers return cell-wise refinement decisions. The refinement
dispatch of every solver turns these decisions into
peano4::RefinementRequest records, and exahype2::RefinementRequests
gives every rank the same request set.

# Enabling adaptive levels

The project configuration supplies a regular base level and a maximum level.
Adaptivity is active when the maximum exceeds the base. Each additional level
divides the cell width by the subdivision factor of the active curve.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
exahype2Project = exahype2.Project(
  ...,
  min_grid_levels=baseLevels,
  max_grid_levels=maximumLevels,
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The solver installs its criterion through `set_pde_terms()`:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver.set_pde_terms(
  ...,
  refinement_criterion=True,
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The generated solver expects a `refinementCriterion()` implementation that
returns exahype2::RefinementCommand::Refine,
exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep, or
exahype2::RefinementCommand::Erase.

# Decision semantics

`Refine` names the current cell and requests one finer neighbourhood around
it. The configured maximum level suppresses requests from cells that are
already sufficiently fine. `Keep` emits no structural request. `Erase`
contributes one vote towards removing the current cell's sibling group.

Erasure requires agreement from all children of the parent and all
participating solvers. exahype2::RefinementRequests promotes a complete
sibling vote to the parent cell. Peano then removes the associated plan flags
only when the whole adjacent parent-cell ring agrees and no refine request
protects it.

# Distributed requests

Each rank evaluates the criterion on its owned cells in one dispatch after
the time step. The refinement requests gather, sort and deduplicate the
committed records, so all ranks evaluate the same vector and independently
derive the same target tree, ownership table, rank-boundary order and
migration.

A solver that participates in the refinement contributes its decision for
every evaluated cell. Omitting a solver's erase vote prevents the sibling
consensus required for coarsening.

# Regrids

The time loop runs the dynamic-AMR phase after a step commits requests. The
spacetree adopts the plan, rebuilds the cell connectivity, and every solver's
`regrid()` moves its data through the peano4::Regrid. The phase repeats
until no request arrives.

A criterion can evaluate only cells that exist. Refinement through several
additional levels therefore requires a new decision after each level appears.
A step deletes the erase request of every cell that the regrid refines for
the second time. A cell refines on its own request at most twice in one
regrid. Every regrid ends after a finite number of steps. The time loop
resumes only after the complete drain settles.

# Solver data transfer

Interpolation initialises data created on a finer level. Restriction
preserves fine-grid information when cells disappear or when coarse and fine
faces exchange data across a level jump. Each solver family owns the
interpolation, restriction, halo and time-stepping policy required by its
data layout: the finite-volume family interpolates and restricts its patches
and rebuilds its face halos, the DG families use the operators of
`exahype2/dg/Basis.h` derived from the quadrature nodes, and the subcell
limiter moves its statuses and subgrids with the cells.

*/
